text:
@sdfggdfg5fgdfg Thank you! I guess I can't really quantify specifically if my peripheral vision "improved" because as you said, with glasses the periphery is just always limited so in the sense that I know can see my entire field of vision clearly, YES it has improved!

One time during a league final for grass volleyball, I landed a bit rough after a jump and my glasses fell off and essentially got "buried" in the grass and I could not for the life of me find it with my blurred vision. Thankfully the ref called for a redo on the point, but the helpless feeling of suddenly not being able to see definitely made me feel very vulnerable.

I also hated contacts cuz for whatever reason I have the toughest time getting them out if I wear them for more than even like 4 hours. I felt like i was clawing at my eyeballs just to remove them and I used to get so anxious whenever they won't come off. Tried rehydrating them with artificial tears, tried all the techniques and nothing seemed to work. Now I don't have to worry about that anymore, at least for maybe another decade or so hopefully when I will need reading glasses or something. The freedom is indescribable!

